<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>"Why do I have to confess to a priest? Can't I just ask God directly for forgiveness?"</h4>
<p>You can and should ask God directly for forgiveness every day. God always hears. But Christ instituted the sacrament of Penance as a visible and efficacious sign of his forgiveness. It's not a human invention: it's a gift from the Risen Lord. When the priest absolves you, it's not a suggestion or a good wish. It's Christ saying, "Your sins are forgiven," through the minister. Moreover, verbal confession has a psychological and spiritual power that private prayer cannot achieve: bringing sin outside, putting it into words, hearing the voice that says, "You are forgiven." The sacrament gives you certainty. Private prayer leaves you in doubt.</p>

<h4>"I'm ashamed. The priest will think badly of me afterwards."</h4>
<p>First: a good confessor does not judge. His only concern is giving you Christ's peace. Second: priests hear dozens of confessions in a single day. The sins blur together. Honestly, he probably won't remember what you said an hour later. Third and most importantly: even if he did remember, he CANNOT tell anyone. The seal of confession is absolute. If he breaks it, he is automatically excommunicated. Fourth: the confessional with a screen exists precisely for this reason. You don't even have to see the priest's face.</p>

<h4>"I don't have serious sins. Just small things. Why bother with confession?"</h4>
<p>Confession of venial sins is not obligatory, but it is highly recommended. For three reasons: (1) it strengthens against temptations and prevents venial sins from becoming mortal; (2) it refines the conscience and helps you know yourself better; (3) it gives actual graces for daily life that private prayer does not provide. The Church recommends frequent confession even for souls without mortal sins. You don't go to confession only because you're dirty: you go to become stronger.</p>

<h4>"What if I forget to confess a grave sin? Is the confession invalid?"</h4>
<p>If you forgot a grave sin unintentionally &mdash;you didn't deliberately hide it, you genuinely didn't remember it&mdash; the confession is valid and the sin is forgiven. But when you remember it later, you should confess it at your next confession. What invalidates a confession is deliberately hiding a mortal sin out of shame. That is a sacrilege and makes the situation worse. But God is not a trickster looking to invalidate your confession over a genuine oversight. God is a Father, not a prosecutor.</p>

<h4>"Can I confess by phone or over the internet?"</h4>
<p>No. The sacrament requires the physical presence of both penitent and minister. It must be face-to-face (or screen-to-face, but in the same space). The reason is theological: the sacrament is a personal, bodily encounter, like all sacraments. However, you can use the internet to prepare: look up examinations of conscience, read the Catechism, pray. But the confession itself must be in person.</p>

<h4>"I'm divorced and remarried civilly. Can I go to confession?"</h4>
<p>You can and should go to confession. The Church welcomes you with love. But in your situation, absolution requires the firm purpose of living in continence (as brother and sister) if you cannot separate for grave reasons (such as children). This is not a punishment: it is coherence with the indissolubility of marriage. Speak with a trusted priest about your specific situation. Each case is different. Do not make decisions without pastoral guidance.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, Good Shepherd,<br />
who seeks not the righteous but the sinner,<br />
who condemns not but saves,<br />
who never tires of forgiving,<br />
even though we tire of asking for forgiveness:<br />
give me your gaze to see each person in this group<br />
as the father of the prodigal son sees his child:<br />
from far off, moved, running.<br />
Give me words that do not accuse but embrace,<br />
that do not frighten but invite.<br />
Let me never think of myself as a judge:<br />
just a bridge between their wounded hearts<br />
and your open Heart.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

